    {
      "id": "5fySkKlNIlPa6jVIhuZGR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -27529.45462210774,
      "y": -591.2958287458882,
      "width": 2067.738525390625,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1157590307,
      "version": 1308,
      "versionNonce": 988485261,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784059427911,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- client has a bucket that can hold a certain nos of tokens(burst capacity), tokens gets added to bucket at steady rate(the refill rate), each request consumes 1 token, if no tokes available request is rejected.\n- capacity = 100, refill rate = 10 tokens/min, a client makes 100 request immediately then must wait for tokens to refill.\n- Handles sustained loads and temp bursts, just track (tokens, last_refill_time) per client\n- Used in production - stripe\n\n- Use Redis to maintain central state for (tokens, last_refill_time) values per client which is shared by all API Gateway instances. client -> LB -> API Gateway(multiple instances) -> Server(multiple instances)\n- User HMGET alice:bucket tokens last_refill, user Redis txn to perform atomic updates using MULTI/EXEC, delete bucket after 1H of inactivity using EXPIRE to prevent memory leaks\n- Race condition could occur if two limiters tries to read at the same time, Move entier read-calculate-update logic to single atomic operation using Lua Scripting\n\n- Perfect for our use case as < 1ms response from redis, auto cleanup, highly available and atomic",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- client has a bucket that can hold a certain nos of tokens(burst capacity), tokens gets added to bucket at steady rate(the refill rate), each request consumes 1 token, if no tokes available request is rejected.\n- capacity = 100, refill rate = 10 tokens/min, a client makes 100 request immediately then must wait for tokens to refill.\n- Handles sustained loads and temp bursts, just track (tokens, last_refill_time) per client\n- Used in production - stripe\n\n- Use Redis to maintain central state for (tokens, last_refill_time) values per client which is shared by all API Gateway instances. client -> LB -> API Gateway(multiple instances) -> Server(multiple instances)\n- User HMGET alice:bucket tokens last_refill, user Redis txn to perform atomic updates using MULTI/EXEC, delete bucket after 1H of inactivity using EXPIRE to prevent memory leaks\n- Race condition could occur if two limiters tries to read at the same time, Move entier read-calculate-update logic to single atomic operation using Lua Scripting\n\n- Perfect for our use case as < 1ms response from redis, auto cleanup, highly available and atomic",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "9Le9qBlDNVdl-g8mIBZdz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -27528.368907406493,
      "y": -218.43699279903035,
      "width": 1735.15869140625,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 12592557,
      "version": 316,
      "versionNonce": 1555893411,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784059658769,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Always \"fail fast\" when limits reached return with 429, don't queue, unnecessary memory, processing, users might think the request failed and retry creating more load\n- Queue requests when batch processing where system can afford to wait\n- When rejecting a request, we return HTTP 429 \"Too Many Requests\" along with headers that help clients understand what happened and how to recover. The key headers are:\n    - X-RateLimit-Limit: The rate limit ceiling for that request (e.g., \"100\")\n    - X-RateLimit-Remaining: Number of requests left in the current window (e.g., \"0\")\n    - X-RateLimit-Reset: When the rate limit resets, as a Unix timestamp (e.g., \"1640995200\")",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Always \"fail fast\" when limits reached return with 429, don't queue, unnecessary memory, processing, users might think the request failed and retry creating more load\n- Queue requests when batch processing where system can afford to wait\n- When rejecting a request, we return HTTP 429 \"Too Many Requests\" along with headers that help clients understand what happened and how to recover. The key headers are:\n    - X-RateLimit-Limit: The rate limit ceiling for that request (e.g., \"100\")\n    - X-RateLimit-Remaining: Number of requests left in the current window (e.g., \"0\")\n    - X-RateLimit-Reset: When the rate limit resets, as a Unix timestamp (e.g., \"1640995200\")",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
