    {
      "id": "UmdpFprhQ63nCa7A_cMDy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24999.5119144577,
      "y": -4076.904659227709,
      "width": 1705.5987548828125,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aL",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1169587555,
      "version": 606,
      "versionNonce": 2135527491,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784061235755,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale to 1 Million req/sec?\n- Single Redis can handle 100k-200k operation/sec but we have multiple operations in a single txn so we only have 50k-100k operations/sec so multiple Redis instance needed.\n- Use Redis cluster which automatically handle shards for you and has 16384 hash slots\n- We roughly need 10 Redis shards to handle 100k operations/sec",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale to 1 Million req/sec?\n- Single Redis can handle 100k-200k operation/sec but we have multiple operations in a single txn so we only have 50k-100k operations/sec so multiple Redis instance needed.\n- Use Redis cluster which automatically handle shards for you and has 16384 hash slots\n- We roughly need 10 Redis shards to handle 100k operations/sec",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "67DpSkaWsxdfj3i0QXB-q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24989.88340239118,
      "y": -3917.50498354663,
      "width": 1752.558837890625,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aM",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2040808589,
      "version": 715,
      "versionNonce": 230086509,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784061630725,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure high availability and fault tolerance?\n- Highly dependent on Redis, if it goes down\n    - Fail Closed - reject all requests with HTTP 503 \"Service Unavailable\" or 429, effectively takes our API down\n    - Fail Open - allow all requests to proceed as if rate limiting was disabled, this means temporarily loosing rate limiting protection, cause cascade failures as backend overwhelmed\n- Select one based on your use-case, for social media use Fail closed protection our BE during viral events\n- But this is all backups, must ensure Redis is highly available using replicas",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure high availability and fault tolerance?\n- Highly dependent on Redis, if it goes down\n    - Fail Closed - reject all requests with HTTP 503 \"Service Unavailable\" or 429, effectively takes our API down\n    - Fail Open - allow all requests to proceed as if rate limiting was disabled, this means temporarily loosing rate limiting protection, cause cascade failures as backend overwhelmed\n- Select one based on your use-case, for social media use Fail closed protection our BE during viral events\n- But this is all backups, must ensure Redis is highly available using replicas",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AMwSs_0WQLo8EoM4KoBHB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24989.88340239118,
      "y": -3713.2979135339324,
      "width": 2925.477783203125,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 505746061,
      "version": 696,
      "versionNonce": 402416941,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784061990885,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to minimise latency overhead?\n- Use connection pooling - instead of establishing new TCP each time to connect to Redis, our Gateway maintains a pool of persistent connections, eliminating TCP handshakes(saving 20-50ms depending on network distance), Redis client handles connection pooling automatically, just tune the pool.\n- Deploy Rate Limiting Infra closer to user's geographic location, trade-off is of data consistency b/w different regions but its fine with eventual consistency b/w regions for lower latency.\n- local caching of rate limit state but is risky to staleness",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to minimise latency overhead?\n- Use connection pooling - instead of establishing new TCP each time to connect to Redis, our Gateway maintains a pool of persistent connections, eliminating TCP handshakes(saving 20-50ms depending on network distance), Redis client handles connection pooling automatically, just tune the pool.\n- Deploy Rate Limiting Infra closer to user's geographic location, trade-off is of data consistency b/w different regions but its fine with eventual consistency b/w regions for lower latency.\n- local caching of rate limit state but is risky to staleness",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_ntvJ5f55RLzNgPwLMwIb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24980.785067588637,
      "y": -3554.5825175339664,
      "width": 1341.7591552734375,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1171504899,
      "version": 1013,
      "versionNonce": 29756291,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784062401694,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle hot keys(viral content scenarios)?\n- This means a single IP or user generates enough requests to overwhelm a Redis shard i.e. 1k request/sec/user\n- This could be DDoS attach, misconfigured bots, or could be from legitimate high volume clients like analytics system, data pipelines etc.\n- For abusive Traffic :\n    - Automatic Blocking: Say client hits rate limit 10 times in a min, temporarily block them by adding them to a block list in Redis\n    - DDoS protection : use Cloudflare or AWS Shield\n- For genuine high volume usecases\n    - Client-side rate limiting: know clients build their own rate limiter on client side.\n    - Request queuing/batching\n    - Offer premium tiers for such users i.e. a rule to have high limits to such users.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle hot keys(viral content scenarios)?\n- This means a single IP or user generates enough requests to overwhelm a Redis shard i.e. 1k request/sec/user\n- This could be DDoS attach, misconfigured bots, or could be from legitimate high volume clients like analytics system, data pipelines etc.\n- For abusive Traffic :\n    - Automatic Blocking: Say client hits rate limit 10 times in a min, temporarily block them by adding them to a block list in Redis\n    - DDoS protection : use Cloudflare or AWS Shield\n- For genuine high volume usecases\n    - Client-side rate limiting: know clients build their own rate limiter on client side.\n    - Request queuing/batching\n    - Offer premium tiers for such users i.e. a rule to have high limits to such users.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ujanWH-XOJemV_DKqH0Mg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -24965.7475420122,
      "y": -3230.075242909826,
      "width": 1660.8388671875,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aP",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 260953421,
      "version": 1201,
      "versionNonce": 1271970189,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784063087857,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle dynamic rule configuration?\n- Production system needs flexibility to adjust limits without deploying new code, say temporarily increasing the limit on black friday etc.\n- i.e. configs/rules needs to be read from a config source\n\n1. Good Solution : Poll-Based Configuration\n- store rule configs in a DB or a dedicated config service, your API gateway periodically polls for config changes(say 30s) and update their rate limiting logic accordingly.\n- The config service can be a simple table with columns for client_type, endpoint, requests_per_minute etc.\nProblem : update delay, i.e. rule updated but need to wait 30s to get it reflected. Say ddos attack and need to reduce limits immediately\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Push-Based Configuration\n- Use a push-based system where configuration changes are immediately sent to all API gateways.\n- i.e. use Zookeeper - a distributed config management with real-time notification.\n- ZooKeeper maintains configuration data and notifies all connected clients (your API gateways) immediately when any configuration changes.\n- Or user etcd or Redis pub/sub\nProblem : Adds complexity to the system.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle dynamic rule configuration?\n- Production system needs flexibility to adjust limits without deploying new code, say temporarily increasing the limit on black friday etc.\n- i.e. configs/rules needs to be read from a config source\n\n1. Good Solution : Poll-Based Configuration\n- store rule configs in a DB or a dedicated config service, your API gateway periodically polls for config changes(say 30s) and update their rate limiting logic accordingly.\n- The config service can be a simple table with columns for client_type, endpoint, requests_per_minute etc.\nProblem : update delay, i.e. rule updated but need to wait 30s to get it reflected. Say ddos attack and need to reduce limits immediately\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Push-Based Configuration\n- Use a push-based system where configuration changes are immediately sent to all API gateways.\n- i.e. use Zookeeper - a distributed config management with real-time notification.\n- ZooKeeper maintains configuration data and notifies all connected clients (your API gateways) immediately when any configuration changes.\n- Or user etcd or Redis pub/sub\nProblem : Adds complexity to the system.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
